		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=39427#p39427"><![CDATA[
Working through the gradient calculation using the Strain Based Dynamics approach, here's what I got (first time writing LaTeX, it's kinda funky):<br><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/download/file.php?id=1539" class="postimage" alt="partial derivative deformation.png" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div>There's a problem here once you start using the SPH weights. <br><br>Depending on how you decide to apply it, is it the weight calculated for the predicted position? The current position? <br><br>Or maybe multiply each column of <strong class="text-strong">Y</strong> by the weight at the predicted position and each column of <strong class="text-strong">X</strong> by the weight at the current position?<br><br>If you multiply <strong class="text-strong">Y</strong> by the predicted weight than the above derivation falls apart because each column of <strong class="text-strong">XY</strong> depends on all 3 components, instead of giving you clean columns like above. I guess that's not a big deal, I might try both ways and see which gives good results.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=12201">ikedude</a> — Mon Jun 05, 2017 6:36 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
